# Windows — Policy Analyzer

---

## Tool

**Microsoft Policy Analyzer** (part of the free Microsoft Security Compliance Toolkit — SCT)

---

## Download

```
https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/download/details.aspx?id=55319
```
When to download: Before your first Windows gap analysis.

---

## Step-by-Step Process

**Step 1 — Download and extract SCT:**
```
Download SCT → Extract PolicyAnalyzer.zip and the baseline ZIP
Example baseline: Windows Server 2019 v1809
```
What it does: Gives you the tool and the recommended settings for your OS version.
When to do this: Match the baseline to your exact OS version and build.

---

**Step 2 — Run PolicyAnalyzer.exe:**
```
Run PolicyAnalyzer.exe as Administrator
```
What it does: Opens the comparison tool that reads your live system settings.
When to do this: On the system you want to audit.

---

**Step 3 — Load the baseline:**
```
Load the baseline folder (usually the Documentation subfolder with .policyrules files)
```
What it does: Loads Microsoft's recommended security settings.

---

**Step 4 — View recommended settings:**
```
Click "View / Compare" → See all recommended baseline settings
```
What it does: Shows you what Microsoft recommends for your OS.

---

**Step 5 — Compare to your live system:**
```
Click "Compare to Effective State" → Side-by-side view
```
What it does: Shows Baseline (recommended) vs Current (real) values side by side.
What to look for: **Yellow highlights = gaps / non-compliance**

---

**Step 6 — Review important gaps:**
```
LockoutBadCount: Baseline = 10 → Effective = 0 (very insecure)
MinimumPasswordLength: Baseline = 14 → Effective = 7 (weak passwords)
```
What it means: Your system doesn't match the recommended security settings.

---

**Step 7 — Fix the gaps:**
```
Export the comparison table → Remediate using:
- Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc)
- LGPO.exe (included in SCT)
```
When to do this: After reviewing which gaps are relevant to your environment.

---

